uldn't target cannons because the  chances of them emerging victorious will  quite slim but what about those parents  who were brave enough or stupid enough  to rob one of these ships didn't they  then go and bury their treasure the  answer to that question is most likely  no pirates had a very short life  expectancy so planning for the future  wasn't something that really crossed  their mind selling the loot as quickly  as possible before the pissed-off  parties could find them was the easiest  and safest solution so if pirates didn't  store treasure then how do they make  their money back in the 18th century  gems and gold are not the only  sought-after an expensive commodity  oriental spices and sugar or sign of  prestige a moth in Europe so they  fetched a small fortune it was also much  easier for pirates to target these small  immersion ships as they would offer  little or no resistance when boarded now  for those of you expecting these epic  war choreographed battles during the  Bourdon of a ship where there's a ton of  back-and-forth the new jacks  comes in dances around 50 men before  killing them all and then jumps in a  cannon and fires himself for the moon  that didn't happen most parts themselves  are not well trained swordsman the what  they lacked in skill they made up for an  aggression barbarity and downright  discuss in this some of you may be  familiar with the UFC where you end up  in a scenario where one fighter not so  the one down and then proceeds the  furrow hammer fits into the unconscious  men skull and this goes over into the  referee decides that his limp lifeless  body cannot continue well that's  probably what most part combat resembled  except there was no referee it was  fairly common for the crew of these  merchant ships just to surrender  immediately when boarded because of how  intimidating his pirates were and after  all this isn't the movies where one  precise stab would kill you it's likely  you'd be bludgeon with some blunt  o